  -- p0 [text L0] Everybody knows the feeling of being stressed. However, when one is asked to describe this feelin
  INSERT INTO paragraphs (section_id, content, content_text, order_index, has_animation, animation_id, animation_trigger, is_subsection_header, subsection_level)
  VALUES (v_section_id, $ob${"blocks":[{"type":"text","content":"Everybody knows the feeling of being stressed. However, when one is asked to describe this feeling in more detail, it becomes obvious how washed-out the meaning of the word really is. Most of the time, it is used in a wide variety of situations and contexts, without giving it a second thought. However, when it comes down to a precise definition of the term, it becomes clear how diverse the perception of stress can be. For some, a daily commute in a crowded subway is stressful, while others remain calm and sometimes even enjoy the busy world around them. Some people only live up to their full potential when up on a stage speaking to a large crowd, whereas others break out in a sweat the moment they think about a similar situation. The 'why' and 'how' of stress have puzzled doctors and scientists for decades and continue to drive research today. This chapter explores the multifaceted nature of stress, examining its historical and evolutionary roots, its biological and neurological underpinnings, and its profound impact on health and well-being."}]}$ob$::jsonb, $ob$Everybody knows the feeling of being stressed. However, when one is asked to describe this feeling in more detail, it becomes obvious how washed-out the meaning of the word really is. Most of the…$ob$, 0, false, NULL, NULL, false, 0);
  -- p1 [text L0] Nowadays, it is clear that stress is a biological phenomenon, but 200 years ago this insight was 
  INSERT INTO paragraphs (section_id, content, content_text, order_index, has_animation, animation_id, animation_trigger, is_subsection_header, subsection_level)
  VALUES (v_section_id, $ob${"blocks":[{"type":"text","content":"Nowadays, it is clear that stress is a biological phenomenon, but 200 years ago this insight was missing (Figure 1). One of the first scientists working on the concept of stress was the French physiologist Claude Bernard, who in the middle-to-late 19<sup>th</sup> century defined his theory of the <em>milieu intérieur</em>, stating that the maintenance of life depends crucially on keeping our internal environment constant in the face of a changing external environment (Bernard, 1927). Fifty years later, Walter Cannon expanded on Bernard’s ideas about internal physiological mechanisms in response to toxic influences and introduced the term <em>homeostasis</em> (Cannon, 1929). This drive for internal stability, crucial for survival, has its roots in our evolutionary history, where organisms constantly faced threats and challenges. If faced with a predator, for example, it is highly beneficial for an individual to prepare for a ‘fight or flight’ response to increase their chances of survival and individual fitness. Yet, it was not until the 1930s that Canadian-Hungarian scientist Hans Selye (1907-1982) coined the term <em>stress response</em> and linked stress to physiological conditions that could be described and explained by biological processes (Selye, 1936). Building on the work of Walter Cannon, Selye proposed a now-famous hypothesis of the <em>general adaptation syndrome</em> (Szabo et al., 2012). Selye stated that the body always tries to retain a balance in its physiological parameters (pH, glucose levels, etc.) in order to maintain an overall healthy condition. Once this homeostasis is disturbed by external factors, the body starts to send alarm signals in order to restore its original state. Selye described the three steps involved in the process as the alarm reaction, the resistance stage, and ultimately, the exhaustion stage. Furthermore, Selye highlighted the impact of both positive (eustress) and negative (distress) stressors on organisms. In fact, often overlooked, eustress (e.g., the stress of creation, whether in art or science) as Seyle described it, influences motivation, alertness, and attention, and is an essential factor for health and survival. Distress, as the name suggests, is associated with negative phenomena like fear or anxiety, which can potentially lead to pathological responses to stressors. This relationship was originally described by psychologists Robert Yerkes and John Dodson in 1908 as a hypothetical concept, and later expanded upon by others. An early definition that was used by Selye in an interview with Jerry Jarvis in 1974 described stress as “the non-specific response of the body to any demand made on it”."}]}$ob$::jsonb, $ob$Nowadays, it is clear that stress is a biological phenomenon, but 200 years ago this insight was missing (Figure 1). One of the first scientists working on the concept of stress was the French…$ob$, 1, false, NULL, NULL, false, 0);
  -- p2 [figure 1] Figure 1. Timeline: how the idea of stress developed.
  INSERT INTO paragraphs (section_id, content, content_text, order_index, has_animation, animation_id, animation_trigger, is_subsection_header, subsection_level)
  VALUES (v_section_id, $ob${"blocks":[{"type":"figure_placeholder","number":1,"caption":"Timeline: how the idea of stress developed."},{"type":"text","content":". Timeline: how the idea of stress developed."}]}$ob$::jsonb, $ob$Figure 1. Timeline: how the idea of stress developed.$ob$, 2, true, v_fig_1, 'auto', false, 0);
  -- p3 [text L0] Expanding on Selye's foundational work, researchers further explored the dynamic nature of stress
  INSERT INTO paragraphs (section_id, content, content_text, order_index, has_animation, animation_id, animation_trigger, is_subsection_header, subsection_level)
  VALUES (v_section_id, $ob${"blocks":[{"type":"text","content":"Expanding on Selye's foundational work, researchers further explored the dynamic nature of stress adaptation. Sterling and Eyer postulated that the needs and optimal conditions of an organism tend to change over the course of its lifespan, and named this phenomenon <em>allostasis</em> (Sterling and Eyer, 1988). Allostasis refers to the body's ability to achieve stability through change, adapting its physiological processes to meet the demands of different situations. This contrasts with the concept of homeostasis, which emphasizes maintaining a fixed internal state. Allostasis recognizes that optimal functioning requires flexibility and adjustments in response to various challenges. For example, heart rate and blood pressure increase during exercise to meet the body's increased energy demands, and cortisol levels rise in the morning to promote alertness and prepare for the day. However, when allostatic systems are repeatedly activated or fail to shut off properly, it can lead to wear and tear on the body, known as <em>allostatic load</em>. Allostatic load is the cumulative cost of chronic exposure to fluctuating or heightened physiological responses to stress. This can manifest in various ways, such as chronically elevated blood pressure, dysregulated cortisol levels, or impaired immune function. Bruce McEwen was one of the most influential scientists to further develop these ideas, emphasizing the brain's role in allostasis and the impact of stress on various brain regions and functions (McEwen, 2000). He highlighted how chronic stress can lead to structural and functional changes in the brain, contributing to cognitive decline, emotional dysregulation, and increased vulnerability to mental illness (see Box 1)."}]}$ob$::jsonb, $ob$Expanding on Selye's foundational work, researchers further explored the dynamic nature of stress adaptation. Sterling and Eyer postulated that the needs and optimal conditions of an organism tend…$ob$, 3, false, NULL, NULL, false, 0);
  -- p4 [text L0] Another important focus of interest was the classification of different kinds of stressors and th
  INSERT INTO paragraphs (section_id, content, content_text, order_index, has_animation, animation_id, animation_trigger, is_subsection_header, subsection_level)
  VALUES (v_section_id, $ob${"blocks":[{"type":"text","content":"Another important focus of interest was the classification of different kinds of stressors and their impact on health and disease. These stressors differ in causality, severity, and modality; physical stressors, for example, trigger different pathways than anticipatory stressors, thereby evoking a variety of adaptive responses from the body. To effectively respond to a stressor, the body must first assess the situation using sensory input and memory. It then initiates a physiological response, activating necessary systems, and finally returns to a non-stress state once the threat has passed. However, while our stress response system has evolved to deal with acute physical stressors, it is nowadays often activated by chronic psychological stressors, in which the physiological adaptations of the stress response may be less adaptive and can even lead to health issues and pathology."}]}$ob$::jsonb, $ob$Another important focus of interest was the classification of different kinds of stressors and their impact on health and disease. These stressors differ in causality, severity, and modality;…$ob$, 4, false, NULL, NULL, false, 0);
  -- p5 [text L0] Even though the initial definition of stress has been around for over 80 years, there is still an
  INSERT INTO paragraphs (section_id, content, content_text, order_index, has_animation, animation_id, animation_trigger, is_subsection_header, subsection_level)
  VALUES (v_section_id, $ob${"blocks":[{"type":"text","content":"Even though the initial definition of stress has been around for over 80 years, there is still an ongoing discussion about the exact description of stress. The understanding of stress and its definition is changing over time, which is influenced by the increased attention to stress-related disorders within society. A review from Richter-Levin &amp; Sandi highlights the increased association of stress exposure with the development of psychopathology, even though the more common reaction towards stress exposure is adaptation and building resiliency towards stress (Richter-Levin and Sandi, 2021). This has led to a proposed distinction between <em>stress</em> and <em>stressful experiences</em>. <em>Stress</em> would then refer to adaptive and resilient responses, while <em>trauma</em> would denote exposures leading to negative consequences like psychopathology. It is important to recognize that the definition of stress has a significant impact on society, and that ongoing discussions about its definition are crucial to reflect evolving societal understanding."}]}$ob$::jsonb, $ob$Even though the initial definition of stress has been around for over 80 years, there is still an ongoing discussion about the exact description of stress. The understanding of stress and its…$ob$, 5, false, NULL, NULL, false, 0);
  -- p6 [text L0] In the following chapters, we will dive into the underlying biology of stress, and its regulation
  INSERT INTO paragraphs (section_id, content, content_text, order_index, has_animation, animation_id, animation_trigger, is_subsection_header, subsection_level)
  VALUES (v_section_id, $ob${"blocks":[{"type":"text","content":"In the following chapters, we will dive into the underlying biology of stress, and its regulation by brain circuits and neurotransmitters. We will also discuss the impact of stress on our mental and physical health, the role of stress during early development, and end with a discussion of strategies for stress management and intervention."}]}$ob$::jsonb, $ob$In the following chapters, we will dive into the underlying biology of stress, and its regulation by brain circuits and neurotransmitters. We will also discuss the impact of stress on our mental and…$ob$, 6, false, NULL, NULL, false, 0);

  -- Section 1: The Biology of Stress
